divinity original sin - is a huge game  with a fair few challenges so obviously  we weren't going to be able to cover all  the tips in just one video thankfully  you clever lot left tons of great  suggestions in the comments of our  beginner's guide so here are seven more  tips to help you through divinity  original sin - as smooth move notes one  of the changes from the previous game is  that MPC's will notice when their  belongings go missing if you pickpocket  someone they'll Pat themselves down and  look around pointing the finger at you  if you're lingering if you pilfer a room  someone will come check out where their  prized painting is vanished - so make  sure you flee the scene of the crime or  be prepared to chat fight or bribe your  way out of the problem a side note about  stealing and thievery if you find a  chest that you can't lockpick just take  the whole down thing with you and open  it a few levels later  [Music]  a tip from Sondra Heian definitely  pronounce that wrong is to flee battles  if you're getting your god broken ass  handed to you enemies aren't scaled to  your level so you may come across bad  guys that will stomp you in one hit if  that's the case use the flee option get  out of Dodge and come back when you've  got another skill point or two under  your belt a very useful tip from say  Emma is how to effectively position your  party you can unchain character so you  can control them independently this is  super useful for getting your party into  advantageous spots sneak your rogue  behind an enemy for an easy backstab or  your spell caster or Wayfarer are  somewhere up high so they can get damage  boost once the fireballs and crossbow  bolts start flying the actual damage and  saved action points can really give you  the edge in some tougher fights no  veneer he'll correctly points out the  skeletons have an extra trick up their  sleeves their bony fingers can be used  instead of lockpicks meaning that you  can sell any real lockpicks to traders  for more potions and gear even if your  skeleton is a spellcaster it's worth a  level or two in thievery or equipping  them with an item that boost the thief  skill just so they can make the most  other pointy digits side note while  we're talking about fingers and hands  lizards can dig stuff up so no worries  if there's buried treasure and you're  short a shovel as Seraph PR mentions in  their comment teleporting can be  incredibly useful both in combat and  outside in the world in the first area  of the game you can find gloves of  teleportation which while not great when  it comes to armor stats do give you the  ability to teleport in combat this is  great for getting members of your party  into advantageous positions or out of  harm's way but my favorite thing to do  is teleport that pesky spell caster  right between your two melee fighters  when you're out in the world you can use  the ability to reach some faraway  treasure or teleport yourself so gaps or  even out of jail cells and keep your  eyes open for the teleporter pyramids at  the end of Act one you can really get  creative with a pair of these bad boys  sneaking them into hard-to-reach spaces  or balancing between party members  almost feels like cheating and since  your inventories are basically magic you  can move them between party members when  were miles apart  Catharine FM commented that it's  important to use your map effectively if  you've been playing divinity original  sin - you'll know it's a hugely dense  game there's no wasted space here  nearly every spot of the map has  something for you to find with that  being said you'll sometimes find  tempting excursions that you just aren't  ready for it may be a fight that's too  tough right now or some treasure you  can't unlock yet the game won't hold  your hand to make a note before you so  be sure to make a mark on your map of  what discovery you've made and come back  when you're ready for it be a locked  door a tricky dungeon and NPC are having  trouble persuading instead of wandering  around thinking I'm sure there was  something here jot it down in your map  just like you're in the scouts the final  tip that we have is to make sure you're  using the right person for the  conversation some characters will have  unique dialogue choices that you should  make the most of but more importantly  you should try to ensure that the person  doing the talking is the right party  member for the job use the character  with the best persuasion stat if the NPC  you're trying to convince as being a  stick-in-the-mud and if you're wondering  around leading a party with your main  character it's easy to forget that they  may not be the best choice when it comes  to talking to merchants so take note of  who's got the best stats and things like  bartering and persuasion so you can  charm your way to victory or save  yourself a few gold pieces so those are  some more tips that will hopefully help  you try them from the world of River lon  if you've got any more to add the  weirder the better make sure to leave  them in the comment section below and  they may just make it into the next  video if you want some more beginners  tips then make sure you check out our  previous guide and for more content make  sure you subscribe to GameSpot  you
 

